# The committed HTML files that carry `?v=` cache-busting pins.
#
# Those hashes are rebuilt from whatever CSS and JS the build produced, so any
# two branches that touch styling collide here even when neither one edited a
# word of markup — it was most of the conflict traffic while shipping the Top
# Menu work on 2026-08-11, and the fix was mechanical every single time.
#
# scripts/merge_asset_pins.cjs merges these by ignoring the pins and putting
# them back per asset afterwards. A genuine markup conflict still conflicts.
#
# The driver itself is defined in .git/config, which is NOT committed —
# scripts/install_git_hooks.cjs registers it, and npm `prepare` runs that on
# every install. Without that step git silently falls back to the default
# merge, which is the old behaviour rather than a broken one.
src/layout.html             merge=asset-pins
public/about.html           merge=asset-pins
public/site.html            merge=asset-pins
public/builder-preview.html merge=asset-pins
public/explore.html         merge=asset-pins

# Adding a committed HTML file that carries a `?v=` pin? Add it here in the
# same commit. public/explore.html shipped in #301 without this line and the
# very next branch that touched styling hit exactly the conflict this file
# exists to prevent.

# The plain-English work log. Every loop-built PR prepends one dated entry to
# the top of this file, so any two branches open at the same time collide here
# even though neither touched the other's entry — and both entries are always
# keepers. On 2026-08-22 a single merge turned 17 of 23 open PRs from clean to
# conflicting on this file alone, one after another, because each merge
# re-conflicts every branch still waiting.
#
# `union` is git's BUILT-IN driver: on a conflicting hunk it keeps both sides'
# lines instead of writing markers. That is exactly right for a prepend-only
# log — both new entries land at the top, newest-first order is preserved, and
# nothing is dropped. Verified before adding this line: merging main into the
# `main-guard` branch went from one conflicted file to zero, with every heading
# from BOTH sides present in the result.
#
# Unlike the asset-pins driver above, this one needs no registration in
# .git/config and no install step, because git ships it. That also means GitHub
# honours it server-side, so "Update branch" on a pull request stops conflicting.
#
# A genuine edit to the SAME entry by two branches would still be merged by
# keeping both lines rather than flagging it — acceptable here, because entries
# are append-only history that nobody goes back and rewrites.
docs/WORK-LOG.md merge=union
